# ADE — Agentic Development Environment

### Workflow

When asked to implement a feature or fix a bug using the ADE workflow
(triggered by invoking the `ade-pipeline` skill or when the user says "use ADE"):

**Phase 0 — INTENT**: Extract structured requirements from the task:
- Type: feature | bugfix | refactor
- Goal: one-sentence summary
- Acceptance criteria: bullet list of what "done" looks like
- Affected areas: which packages/features are likely impacted
- Estimated scope: S (< 3 files) | M (3-10 files) | L (> 10 files)
- Save to `.ade/tasks/<task-id>/intent.md`

**Routing (closing sub-step of Phase 0):** assign a tier — `trivial` / `standard` /
`architecture` — that masks which phases run. `standard` is the full flow; `trivial` cuts
ceremony (keeps TDD + quality gate + merge gate); `architecture` adds an ADR + a Plan
Soundness Review. Forced-escalation (security/auth/secrets/schema/migration/public-API)
raises the tier deterministically and is enforced by the `check-escalation-paths` hook.
Rules + globs live in `.ade/ade-routing.json`.

**Phase 1 — RESEARCH**: Produce a verified, durable spec in five sub-steps (R1–R5).
The phase accumulates three project artifacts: a permanent spec, an updated domain
glossary (`CONTEXT.md`), and zero-or-more ADRs under `docs/adr/`.

- **R1 Intent**: from Phase 0. Scope estimate (S/M/L) is informational, not routing.
- **R2 Investigate**:
  - R2.1 Code scouting with **iterative retrieval** (max 3 cycles). Dispatch
    `scout` subagents in parallel: `current-state` + `available-surface`.
    Evaluate against relevance scoring (0.8 high / 0.5 medium / 0.2 low).
    Refine terminology and re-dispatch if stop criterion not met.
  - R2.2 Orchestrator **confidence check**: decide if web research is needed.
    Spawn web research only if user opted in OR scout open_questions reference
    external concepts not in CONTEXT.md. Justify the decision in writing.
  - R2.3 Web research (conditional): dispatch `web-researcher` subagents per
    topic. Tier 0 default (WebSearch + WebFetch + Context7). IPI hardening
    enforced in agent definition.
- **R3 Specify**:
  - R3.1 `synthesizer` subagent drafts spec from research bundles.
  - R3.2 Orchestrator interviews user using the **10-category ambiguity taxonomy**:
    Functional Scope, Data Model, UX Flow, Non-Functional, Integration, Edge Cases,
    Constraints, Terminology, Completion Signals, Misc. **One question at a time**,
    multi-choice preferred (recommended + 1 alternative), **hard cap: 5 questions**.
  - Spec written to `docs/specs/{YYYY-MM-DD}_{slug}.spec.md` (permanent).
- **R4 Refine**: invoke vendored `grill-with-docs` against the spec. Always runs;
  trivial when spec uses existing CONTEXT.md vocabulary. Updates CONTEXT.md inline
  and creates ADRs sparingly (3-criteria gate: hard-to-reverse, surprising, real trade-off).
- **R5 Verify (Chain of Verification, factor+revise)**: extract 8–15 verification
  claims from the spec; dispatch `spec-verifier` subagents in parallel — **each
  receives ONE claim only, never the spec text** (structural guarantee of factor+revise).
  Synthesizer Role B revises spec for material discrepancies.

**◆ READY-FOR-DEVELOPMENT GATE ◆**: Present finalized spec + new CONTEXT.md entries +
new ADRs to user. Confirm before proceeding to Phase 2.

**Phase 2 — PLAN**: Write implementation plan to `.ade/tasks/<task-id>/plan.md`
with 6 mandatory sections: Context, Ordered task list, Files to create/modify,
Dependencies, Test strategy, Risk areas. The plan consumes the spec at
`docs/specs/{date}_{slug}.spec.md` as its primary input.

**◆ PLAN GATE ◆**: Verify plan completeness before proceeding.

**Phase 3 — DESIGN CHECK**: Dispatch a Sonnet subagent in a worktree to
create file stubs. Review for plan alignment (max 2 iterations).

**Phase 4 — IMPLEMENT**: test-writer (RED) → implementer (GREEN), author-separated.
Step 4a: `test-writer` subagent writes FAILING tests, commits them alone (`test:`).
Step 4b: one or more `implementer` subagents (disjoint file assignments) make tests GREEN,
commit alone (`feat:`/`fix:`). Build dependencies before dependents (the plan's task DAG
defines order).

**Stack commands:** build/lint/format/test commands live in `.ade/ade-stack.md`
(seeded by `ade init`, user-owned). Phases reference it instead of hardcoding commands.

**Phase 5 — QUALITY GATE**: Dispatch Haiku subagent to run build + tests.
If failures, dispatch Sonnet fixer subagent (max 3 attempts).

**Phase 6 — REVIEW**: Launch 3 parallel Sonnet review subagents:
- Logic: errors, edge cases, null handling, race conditions
- Conventions: project patterns, naming, structure
- Security: OWASP top 10, injection, auth bypass, secrets
Fix all HIGH and MEDIUM findings before proceeding.

**Phase 7 — DOCUMENTATION**: Update docs triggered by code changes:
- New service/provider → update file tree in architecture doc
- New API route → update endpoints table in architecture doc
- New DB table/migration → update data model table in architecture doc
- New shared interface/type → update package description in architecture doc
- New user-facing feature → update `docs/product-capabilities.md`
- Removed dependency/pattern → note removal in architecture doc
- New convention/command/env var → update CLAUDE.md

**Phase 8 — COMMIT & PR**: Stage, commit, push, open PR.

**◆ MERGE GATE ◆**: Present PR to user for review and merge decision.

**Phase 9 — RETROSPECTIVE**: Record metrics to `.ade/tasks/<task-id>/retro.json`, then
**Codify** (standard/architecture tiers): a read-only `compounder` subagent folds review
findings into `docs/review-calibration.md` (frequency orders the corpus, never promotes
severity) and writes a `docs/learnings/{date}_{slug}.md` when the task yielded a transferable
insight. Phase 1 reads `docs/learnings/` back and Phase 6 reads `docs/review-calibration.md`
fresh — the compound loop. Clean up worktree.

### Post-merge: GitHub PR review-and-fix loop

After the PR exists (or for any external PR), `/ade-pr-review [pr]` runs the
`ade-pr-review` skill: dispatches the `pr-reviewer` agent to inspect the PR
through the GitHub MCP server (or `gh` CLI fallback), then dispatches coder
subagents to apply fixes inside the PR's worktree, then re-reviews. Bounded to
3 cycles. Never auto-merges; never force-pushes.

### Circuit Breaker
- Design check: max 2 iterations
- Code→review loop: max 3 cycles
- QA fix: max 3 iterations
- PR review-fix loop: max 3 cycles
- Plan Soundness Review (architecture): max 2 iterations
After any limit, escalate to user. Do NOT retry silently.

### Orchestrator Rules
- The orchestrator NEVER writes application code — only dispatches subagents
- All code changes flow through subagents (Sonnet for coding/review, Haiku for tests)
- Verify subagent output independently — don't trust self-reports
- Update `.ade/tasks/<task-id>/status.md` at each phase transition

### Models
- Orchestration, planning, review, verification: Claude Opus (this session)
- Coding, fixing, design review: Claude Sonnet (subagents)
- Test execution: Claude Haiku (subagents)
